<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yangtze River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yangtze</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yangtze is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, flowing from the Tibetan plateau east to the sea at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>. Its basin became China's economic heartland, producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and other goods. It served as the principal commercial artery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, the defensive barrier of the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>, and the base from which the Ming founder rose to power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yangtze-river.jpg" alt="Yangtze River" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Hitchcock · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dusk_on_the_Yangtze_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
